\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \'a0In number theory, a perfect number is a\'a0
\f1\b \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 positive integer
\f2\b0 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \'a0that is equal to the sum of its proper\'a0
\f1\b \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 positive divisors
\f2\b0 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 , that is, the sum of its positive divisors excluding the number itself (also known as its aliquot sum). 
\f4 \AppleTypeServices \cf2 \cb6 \strokec2 Given number is 6. 1, 2, and 3 are its proper positive divisors, and 1 + 2 + 3 = 6. Equivalently, the number 6 is equal to half the sum of all its positive divisors: ( 1 + 2 + 3 + 6 ) / 2 = 6. Hence 6 is a perfect number.\
