\f2\b0\fs32 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cf7 \cb8 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 The modulo result in Python follows this formula: a % b = a - (b \'d7 floor(a/b))\
For -10 % 3:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf7 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 -10/3 \uc0\u8776  -3.33\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb8 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 floor(-3.33) = -4\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb8 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 -10 - (3 \'d7 -4) = -10 + 12 = 2\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf7 \cb8 This ensures the result is always between 0 and the divisor (3 in this case).\'a0\
\
The formula is part of Python's design decision to make modulo consistent with math's floor division.\
Think of it this way - when you do -10 \'f7 3:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf7 \cb8 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 The closest multiple of 3 less than -10 is -12 (-4 \'d7 3)\cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0\cb8 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 So, -10 = -12 + 2\cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0\cb8 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec7 That 2 becomes your modulo result\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf7 \cb8 \strokec7 This makes modulo operations consistent across all programming languages that use floor division.\'a0\
\
\
